{"abstract":"Research on scientific careers finds a mover’s advantage. International migration correlates with increased visibility and productivity. However, if scientists who move internationally, on average, enter into more prestigious employments than they came from, extant research may overestimate the direct performance gains associated with international moves. Building on insights from the sociology of science and studies of international researcher mobility, we examine how changes in employment prestige shape international movers’ performance returns to mobility. We follow a cohort of 167,014 European scientists to identify individuals that move to the USA and pair these migrants to non-mobile scientists with identical home institutions, research fields, and genders, giving a final sample of 3978 researchers. Using a difference-in-differences design, we show a substantial increase in the publishing rates and scientific impact of transatlantic migrants, compared to non-mobile scientists. However, most of the movers’ mobility-related boost in citation and journal impact is attributable to changes in employment prestige. In contrast, we find limited effects of employment prestige on changes in migrants’ publication rates. Overall, our study suggests large variations in the outcomes of transatlantic migration and reaffirms the citation-related “visibility advantage” tied to prestigious institutional locations.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Holding, Benjamin C.","Acciai, Claudia","Schneider, Jesper Wiborg","Nielsen, Mathias W."],"cuid":["S02-100.00-741814"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Nielsen, Mathias W."],"keyword":["Employment prestige","Performance","Researcher mobility","Science"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"quantifying the mover s advantage transatlantic migration employment prestige and scientific performance","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10734-023-01089-7","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-741814","identifier":[{"id":"85168613406","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-511060a0-2c40-4d99-8df1-a584661260c6","ku-1cd18cc8-0185-4876-8b12-a166494473fb"],"keyword":["Employment prestige","Performance","Researcher mobility","Science"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-4065CA240F8813AA1538975EFB963848"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/366542160/Quantifying_the_mover_s_advantage_transatlantic_migration_employment_prestige_and_scientific_performance.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10734-023-01089-7","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Sociology, Department of Sociology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Aarhus BSS, Department of Political Science, Department of Political Science - Danish Centre for Studies in Research and Research Policy"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Benjamin C.","key":"holdingpau","last":"Holding","locid":"0aada100-4f68-42f6-9dc3-5bd747eb7896","name":"Holding, Benjamin C.","orcid":"0000-0002-8204-6437","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Claudia","key":"acciaipau","last":"Acciai","locid":"39f435a5-2b3c-486d-b48d-7cb6a814a000","name":"Acciai, Claudia","orcid":"0000-0001-6779-2653","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper W.","key":"schneiderpau","last":"Schneider","locid":"8fcbf953-b83f-4fe7-b698-d4aec218e1f5","name":"Schneider, Jesper Wiborg","name_org":"Schneider, Jesper W.","orcid":"0000-0001-5556-0919","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Mathias W.","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"1d5b2521-2997-4092-9bb9-f5cb8abb4bc8","name":"Nielsen, Mathias Wullum","name_org":"Nielsen, Mathias W.","orcid":"0000-0001-8759-7150","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s10734-023-01089-7","issn":["00181560","1573174X"],"issn_org":["00181560"],"issue":"6","journal":"Higher Education","pages":"1749-1767","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Higher Education","volume":"87"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85168613406"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-511060a0-2c40-4d99-8df1-a584661260c6","ku-1cd18cc8-0185-4876-8b12-a166494473fb"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Quantifying the mover’s advantage : transatlantic migration, employment prestige, and scientific performance","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-741814","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"This chapter introduces the theoretical and empirical topics discussed throughout the chapters of this book. It begins with a conceptual and operational definition of Research & Innovation (R&I) policy, which is defined as a generalized case of complex governance arrangements. Then it moves on to a review of the literature on the determinants of policy design choice. Here the author discusses the relevance of partisan politics, institutional layouts, and policy takers’ responsiveness for R&I policy design. The last section presents an overview of the book, where the contents of the following chapters are summarized, and the main arguments of the research are presented.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Acciai, Claudia"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-695506"],"filters":{"added":"2023-11","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"policy design for research and innovation politics institutions and interest intermediation practices","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"db","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-695506","identifier":[{"id":"85172812635","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-1bd20c4c-e0ac-48f6-8169-df9fe03d789b"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-37B441EE83B8365ED7634C3896F74729"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Sociology, Department of Sociology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Claudia","key":"acciaipau","last":"Acciai","locid":"39f435a5-2b3c-486d-b48d-7cb6a814a000","name":"Acciai, Claudia","orcid":"0000-0001-6779-2653","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-36628-4","eisbn":["9783031366284"],"isbn":["9783031366277"],"issn":["25247301"],"pages":"257","place":"Cham","publisher":"Palgrave Macmillan","series":"International Series on Public Policy","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-1bd20c4c-e0ac-48f6-8169-df9fe03d789b"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Policy Design for Research and Innovation : Politics, Institutions and Interest Intermediation Practices","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-695506","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Open data sharing is critical for scientific progress. Yet, many authors refrain from sharing scientific data, even when they have promised to do so. Through a preregistered, randomized audit experiment (N = 1,634), we tested possible ethnic, gender and status-related bias in scientists' data-sharing willingness. 814 (54%) authors of papers where data were indicated to be 'available upon request' responded to our data requests, and 226 (14%) either shared or indicated willingness to share all or some data. While our preregistered hypotheses regarding bias in data-sharing willingness were not confirmed, we observed systematically lower response rates for data requests made by putatively Chinese treatments compared to putatively Anglo-Saxon treatments. Further analysis indicated a theoretically plausible heterogeneity in the causal effect of ethnicity on data-sharing. In interaction analyses, we found indications of lower responsiveness and data-sharing willingness towards male but not female data requestors with Chinese names. These disparities, which likely arise from stereotypic beliefs about male Chinese requestors' trustworthiness and deservingness, impede scientific progress by preventing the free circulation of knowledge.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Acciai, Claudia","Schneider, Jesper Wiborg","Nielsen, Mathias W."],"cuid":["S02-100.00-666293"],"filters":{"added":"2023-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"estimating social bias in data sharing behaviours an open science experiment","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-023-02129-8","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-666293","identifier":[{"id":"85153541543","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC10120507","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"37085512","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-6dcf76b2-9da2-4d5c-b412-bfd1be71f70e","ku-daab7c84-facd-4306-a055-307c67c3e78f"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-41EA81B0505CAECB622CA626C7F7DF1A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/346584611/s41597_023_02129_8.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-023-02129-8","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Sociology, Department of Sociology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Aarhus BSS, Department of Political Science, Department of Political Science - Danish Centre for Studies in Research and Research Policy"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Claudia","key":"acciaipau","last":"Acciai","locid":"39f435a5-2b3c-486d-b48d-7cb6a814a000","name":"Acciai, Claudia","orcid":"0000-0001-6779-2653","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper W.","key":"schneiderpau","last":"Schneider","locid":"8fcbf953-b83f-4fe7-b698-d4aec218e1f5","name":"Schneider, Jesper Wiborg","name_org":"Schneider, Jesper W.","orcid":"0000-0001-5556-0919","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Mathias W.","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"1d5b2521-2997-4092-9bb9-f5cb8abb4bc8","name":"Nielsen, Mathias Wullum","name_org":"Nielsen, Mathias W.","orcid":"0000-0001-8759-7150","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37085512"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41597-023-02129-8","issn":["20524463"],"issue":"1","journal":"Scientific Data","pages":"233","paperid":"233","publisher":"Nature Research","status":"p","title":"Scientific Data","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85153541543"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-6dcf76b2-9da2-4d5c-b412-bfd1be71f70e","ku-daab7c84-facd-4306-a055-307c67c3e78f"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Estimating social bias in data sharing behaviours : an open science experiment","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-666293","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"The COVID-19 pandemic elicited a substantial hike in journal submissions and a global push to get medical evidence quickly through the review process. Editorial decisions and peer-assessments were made under intensified time constraints, which may have amplified social disparities in the outcomes of peer-reviewing, especially for COVID-19 related research. This study quantifies the differential impact of the pandemic on the duration of the peer-review process for women and men and for scientists at different strata of the institutional-prestige hierarchy. Using mixed-effects regression models with observations clustered at the journal level, we analysed newly available data on the submission and acceptance dates of 78,085 medical research articles published in 2019 and 2020. We found that institution-related disparities in the average time from manuscript submission to acceptance increased marginally in 2020, although half of the observed change was driven by speedy reviews of COVID-19 research. For COVID-19 papers, we found more substantial institution-related disparities in review times in favour of authors from highly-ranked institutions. Descriptive survival plots also indicated that scientists with prestigious affiliations benefitted more from fast-track peer reviewing than did colleagues from less reputed institutions. This difference was more pronounced for journals with a single-blind review procedure compared to journals with a double-blind review procedure. Gender-related changes in the duration of the peer-review process were small and inconsistent, although we observed a minor difference in the average review time of COVID-19 papers first authored by women and men.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Acciai, Claudia","Holding, Benjamin C.","Schneider, Jesper Wiborg","Nielsen, Mathias W."],"cuid":["S02-087.23-016056"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Acciai, Claudia","Holding, Benjamin C."],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Karolinska Institutet"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"institution and gender related differences in publication speed before and during covid 000000000019","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0277011","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-087.23-016056","identifier":[{"id":"PMC9671365","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85142152942","type":"scopus"},{"id":"36395177","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-2f8523f4-886b-40ea-a3cb-bc498b328598","ku-762ac72f-3116-468d-817b-5965465ecf84"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-C7CCEF2928DE0D43D8B3CDB3BA265C35"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0277011","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/357268159/journal.pone.0277011.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Sociology, Department of Sociology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Aarhus BSS, Department of Political Science, Department of Political Science - Danish Centre for Studies in Research and Research Policy"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Claudia","key":"acciaipau","last":"Acciai","locid":"39f435a5-2b3c-486d-b48d-7cb6a814a000","name":"Acciai, Claudia","orcid":"0000-0001-6779-2653","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Benjamin","key":"holdingpau","last":"Holding","locid":"0aada100-4f68-42f6-9dc3-5bd747eb7896","name":"Holding, Benjamin C.","name_org":"Holding, Benjamin","orcid":"0000-0002-8204-6437","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper W.","key":"schneiderpau","last":"Schneider","locid":"8fcbf953-b83f-4fe7-b698-d4aec218e1f5","name":"Schneider, Jesper Wiborg","name_org":"Schneider, Jesper W.","orcid":"0000-0001-5556-0919","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Mathias Wullum","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"1d5b2521-2997-4092-9bb9-f5cb8abb4bc8","name":"Nielsen, Mathias Wullum","orcid":"0000-0001-8759-7150","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36395177"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0277011","issn":["19326203"],"issue":"11","journal":"PLOS ONE","paperid":"e0277011","publisher":"Public Library of Science","status":"p","title":"PLOS ONE","volume":"17"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85142152942"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-2f8523f4-886b-40ea-a3cb-bc498b328598","ku-762ac72f-3116-468d-817b-5965465ecf84"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Institution and gender-related differences in publication speed before and during COVID-19","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-087.23-016056","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"This article uses a systematic review of the main literature in the field to shed light on different operationalizations of the main classifications of policy instruments. Although the literature offers a large number of instrument taxonomies, many of them act as theoretical guidelines rather than operational concepts that can help to disentangle the different features of governing actions. This article provides a review of the most frequently used policy instrument typologies and, through a meta‐analysis, it analyses how instrument typologies have been differentially adopted to explain real‐world phenomena. The results are a high degree of heterogeneity in citation frequency, the polyhedral nature of the concept of policy instruments, a divide between typologies focused on governmental resources and those focused on drivers of expected behaviour, and ‘labellism’. Thus, what emerges is the urgency of a process of convergence towards a common framework.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Acciai, Claudia","Capano, Giliberto"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-531851"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"policy instruments at work a meta analysis of their applications","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-531851","identifier":[{"id":"85088823435","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-e7c64711-355b-4113-b9f5-102484d7a412"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-5746D4FA8D7B9FA95D0BA4B5C9C43AB4"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Sociology, Department of Sociology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Claudia","key":"acciaipau","last":"Acciai","locid":"39f435a5-2b3c-486d-b48d-7cb6a814a000","name":"Acciai, Claudia","orcid":"0000-0001-6779-2653","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Giliberto","key":"capanopau","last":"Capano","name":"Capano, Giliberto","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/padm.12673","issn":["00333298","14679299"],"issn_org":["00333298"],"journal":"Public Administration","pages":"118-136","status":"p","title":"Public Administration","volume":"99"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-e7c64711-355b-4113-b9f5-102484d7a412"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Policy instruments at work : A meta‐analysis of their applications","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-531851","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"What governments desire to achieve, and how they want to accomplish their goals, represent the core of any policy design process. However, it is still unclear how partisan politics, in its combined effect with pathdependency forces, influence policy makers' choices over alternative instrument mixes. Through a comparative analysis of Research and Innovation (R&I) instrument choices in countries characterized by different paradigmatic models of policy (Italy and France), the paper investigates how the politics of different cabinets influence the formulation of national R&I strategies and the extent to which these decisions are constrained by the legacy of previous choices. By capitalizing on a new proposed treatment of policy instruments, the paper contributes to the definition of the types of tools found in policy mixes, investigating how national R&I instrument mix variations develop. The results of the analysis indicate how the selection of R&I policy instruments does not closely follow traditional left versus right-wing political cleavages, and their evolution is generally influenced by a combination of different context-dependent dynamics. However, when partisan acceptance of consolidated R&I instrument mixes across cabinets is present, this triggers a consolidating effect on pathdependency forces.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Acciai, Claudia"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-439377"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Design","Government ideology","Ideas","Institutions","Policy mix","Qualitative content analysis","Rationales","SYSTEM","Technology"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the politics of research and innovation understanding instrument choices in complex governance environments the case of france and italy","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-439377","identifier":[{"id":"85110382067","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000687830100011","type":"wos"}],"ids":["ku-4d0de285-5f44-4d9c-81bf-f7c942a4da58"],"keyword":["DESIGN","GOVERNMENT IDEOLOGY","IDEAS","INSTITUTIONS","Instrument mix","POLICY MIX","Politics of instrument choices","Qualitative Content Analysis","RATIONALES","Research & Innovation Policy","SYSTEM","TECHNOLOGY"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-03F95740B8E95E11C0001CA4F8A9AD39"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Sociology, Department of Sociology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Claudia","key":"acciaipau","last":"Acciai","locid":"39f435a5-2b3c-486d-b48d-7cb6a814a000","name":"Acciai, Claudia","orcid":"0000-0001-6779-2653","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.respol.2021.104254","issn":["00487333"],"issue":"9","journal":"Research Policy","paperid":"104254","status":"p","title":"Research Policy","volume":"50"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85110382067"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-4d0de285-5f44-4d9c-81bf-f7c942a4da58"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The politics of research and innovation : Understanding instrument choices in complex governance environments-the case of France and Italy","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-439377","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Home ownership is increasingly understood by policy makers and social scientists as a fundamental asset against poverty risks, especially in times of economic uncertainty. However, in several Western countries, home ownership among younger generations appears to be increasingly difficult to achieve, likely a result of growing employment instability and stringent criteria to access credit. This article uses multinomial logistic models and nationally representative EU-SILC data from six European countries to examine (a) to what extent precarious employment among young couples is linked to being a mortgage holder; (b) whether earned income can compensate for employment instability in being a mortgagee; (c) cross-national differences in the relationship between being a mortgage holder, earnings, and employment insecurity. Our results indicate that the higher the levels of employment insecurity, the lower the chances of being a mortgage holder in all countries. Moreover, we find that at a given level of employment insecurity, households with higher levels of earned income have higher chances of being mortgage holders than households with lower earned income. However, while earned income has a stronger effect in achieving a mortgage among couples who have secure employment in Italy, earnings are more important among couples with lower levels of employment security in France, the UK, Spain and Poland. These results suggest that the relationship between social inequalities and housing is partially mediated by the national context.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Acciai, Claudia","Dottisani, Giulia Maria"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-510118"],"filters":{"added":"2022-04","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"two hearts and a loan mortgages employment insecurity and earnings among young couples in six european countries","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/312034387/UrbStud_DottiSani_Acciai_pre_print_2018.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-510118","identifier":[{"id":"85043370777","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-c36fd217-a0dc-4734-a127-bd7584e9c1e3"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-6BFB057E9FAF4D75B66E498775521B5D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/312034387/UrbStud_DottiSani_Acciai_pre_print_2018.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Sociology, Department of Sociology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Claudia","key":"acciaipau","last":"Acciai","locid":"39f435a5-2b3c-486d-b48d-7cb6a814a000","name":"Acciai, Claudia","orcid":"0000-0001-6779-2653","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Giulia Maria","key":"dottisanipau","last":"Dottisani","name":"Dottisani, Giulia Maria","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/0042098017717211","issn":["00420980","1360063X"],"issn_org":["00420980"],"issue":"11","journal":"Urban Studies","pages":"2451-2469","status":"p","title":"Urban Studies","volume":"55"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-c36fd217-a0dc-4734-a127-bd7584e9c1e3"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Two hearts and a loan? Mortgages, employment insecurity and earnings among young couples in six European countries","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-510118","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
